Native firmware tests (no ESP32 required)
========================================

Run all three suites from the repository root after PlatformIO has downloaded
the GFX Library for Arduino dependency. Its font data is used by the poetry
suite; ./pio run fetches it before compiling the firmware.


./scripts/test_native.sh

The script uses a host C++ compiler with warnings treated as errors. It builds
and runs these production-code suites:

- poetry_native.cpp: app state, touch gating, reading layouts, word motion,
  perspective, 0.66x-1.1x fragment scale, breath response, hidden theme menu,
  and saved settings.
- atmosphere_native.cpp: independent colour fields, three themes, grain,
  readable ink, smoothed sound response, and one navigation per strong breath.
- audio_input_native.cpp: the ES8311 module with simulated I2C and I2S. It
  checks staged startup, discarded warmup spikes, normalized RMS, either active
  slot, bounded zero-timeout DMA reads, stale input, stop/cancel/re-enable,
  and cleanup after codec or driver errors.

Arduino, display output, touch, settings, clocks, and audio hardware are stubbed
as needed. These tests do not measure hardware frame rate or prove the physical
microphone and touch screen work.

Browser and generated-library checks:

node --test test/*.test.cjs
python3 scripts/generate_poetry.py --check

Build the ESP32 binary with ./pio run, or use the ESP32 firmware GitHub Actions
workflow on Ubuntu. Download esp32s3-firmware only from a successful run for the
intended commit. See ../docs/FIRMWARE_FLASHING.md for updating from main and
flashing individual binaries while retaining saved settings. The merged image
is for first installs or updates where resetting settings is acceptable.
